==Introduction==

===Abstract===

This document recommends a standard way to back up a wallet's watch-only output descriptor with privacy comparable to
the secret it derives from, by encrypting it to a key deterministically derived from the same seed via
[https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0085.mediawiki BIP-0085]'s existing RSA-GPG application. It fixes the
derivation parameters this use should use, so that independent implementations derive the same key and interoperate,
and recommends when a wallet should offer this instead of, or alongside, plaintext descriptor export.

===Copyright===

This document is licensed under the 3-clause BSD license.

===Motivation===

A watch-only descriptor is enough to monitor a wallet's balance and history without exposing spending keys, and losing
it costs privacy, not funds: extended public keys and script structure are recoverable from the seed at any time, so a
lost descriptor is an inconvenience, not a disaster. Storing it in the clear is a common recommendation, since it
appears to have little to protect.

This is not always true. A descriptor reveals which addresses belong to the same wallet, including future ones, and
for a multisignature wallet it identifies every cosigner's extended public key. A locally-stored, unencrypted
descriptor found in a device search, backup service breach, or estate paperwork discloses this even though no funds
are at risk directly; the same disclosure can motivate a targeted attack against the funds themselves, or against the
people identified as cosigners. Where descriptors are shared between cosigners or a watch-only server, this privacy
loss is multiplied across every location.

The direct fix, storing the descriptor only in memory or destroying it after use, conflicts with the descriptor's own
purpose: watch-only monitoring is meant to be ongoing, not a one-time lookup, and needs a durable copy somewhere.

An earlier discussion of this
tradeoff<ref>'''Prior discussion.''' [https://github.com/BlockstreamResearch/codex32/discussions/65 A 2023 discussion
of backing up codex32 wallets' extended public keys] considered redacting each cosigner's own key from a shared
descriptor for privacy, and concluded that encrypting the complete descriptor to oneself is preferable: redaction
only protects the redacting party, leaks a "public 2-of-2, private 1-of-2" property that participants may not expect,
and does not help a single-signature wallet at all, whereas whole-descriptor encryption applies uniformly and needs no
special handling of participants.</ref> concluded that the descriptor should be encrypted whole, to a key the user can
always reconstruct from their existing seed, rather than redacted or protected by a separately-managed secret. BIP-0085
already defines a suitable key: its RSA-GPG application derives a deterministic GPG-compatible RSA keypair, including a
dedicated encryption subkey, from a BIP-0032 root key. This document fixes the remaining parameters (key size and
index) needed for independent wallets to derive the identical key, and recommends the resulting workflow.

==Specification==

===Key derivation===

Implementations MUST derive the encryption key as the BIP-0085 RSA-GPG encryption subkey at
<code>m/83696968'/828365'/4096'/0'/0'</code>: RSA-GPG application (<code>828365'</code>), 4096-bit key size, key index
0, encryption subkey (<code>0'</code>), as specified in BIP-0085's RSA GPG section. The fixed creation timestamp
specified there (UNIX epoch 1231006505) MUST be used, since the derived key's GPG fingerprint depends on it.

4096 bits is chosen as the only key size this document defines, so that implementations that support only one size
interoperate; see Rationale.

===Encrypting a descriptor===

To back up a descriptor, encrypt its exact text, encoded as UTF-8, to the public part of the key above, using
[https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4880 OpenPGP] (RFC 4880) or a compatible implementation.
Implementations SHOULD prefer an AEAD-protected OpenPGP mode where available, over the older MDC-protected mode<ref>
'''Why prefer AEAD?''' OpenPGP's original integrity mechanism (MDC) has known construction weaknesses; OpenPGP's
current AEAD modes address these. Since this key is used for nothing else, there is no compatibility reason to prefer
the older mode.</ref>. The resulting ciphertext MAY be stored or transmitted like any other encrypted file; unlike the
descriptor's plaintext, its disclosure does not by itself reveal wallet structure.

===Recovering a descriptor===

To recover the descriptor, re-derive the same private key from the same seed and decrypt the ciphertext with any
compatible OpenPGP implementation.

===Wallet behavior===

A wallet that supports this specification SHOULD offer it as the default export option for a watch-only descriptor
whenever the user has not indicated the descriptor may be handled as public information, and MUST make plaintext
export available as an explicit alternative. A wallet MUST warn before plaintext export that the descriptor identifies
every address the wallet will ever use, and, for a multisignature wallet, every cosigner.

==Rationale==

'''Why BIP-0085 rather than a fresh key?''' A key independent of the seed would need its own backup, recreating the
exact backup-multiplication problem this document exists to avoid. A BIP-0085-derived key needs no separate backup: it
is always reconstructable from the same seed the user already protects, using the same care they already give it.

'''Why RSA-GPG specifically, rather than a new derivation and format?''' BIP-0085 already defines this application,
including a subkey designated for encryption; reusing it needs no new cryptographic construction, and produces a
key immediately usable with existing, widely deployed OpenPGP tooling, rather than a bespoke format needing its own
implementation. Native OpenPGP hybrid encryption also removes the need for this document to separately specify a
symmetric cipher, key-wrapping scheme, or ciphertext format.

'''Why fix one key size instead of letting wallets choose?''' BIP-0085's RSA-GPG path already parameterizes key size;
this document does not need to reintroduce that choice, only to fix one value so implementations agree without
negotiation. 4096 bits is chosen as a conservative, widely-supported OpenPGP RSA size. A future version of this
document MAY define additional sizes if 4096-bit RSA generation proves impractical on constrained hardware; see Open
questions.

'''Why key index 0 rather than deriving from wallet-specific data?''' A fixed index keeps the derivation path
independent of the descriptor being protected. Recovery therefore does not depend on remembering, or separately
recording, which index was used.

==Backwards compatibility==

This document defines a new use of already-specified BIP-0085 derivation; it changes nothing about existing BIP-0085
applications, GPG, or descriptors, and a descriptor backed up this way decrypts to byte-identical plaintext with any
compliant OpenPGP implementation.

==Privacy and security considerations==

The encrypted backup reveals nothing about wallet structure, but its mere existence and approximate size may still
disclose that the holder uses Bitcoin and, roughly, how large a descriptor they protect. Where this matters, treat the
ciphertext's storage location with the same consideration given to that metadata for any other encrypted file.

This document inherits BIP-0085's own security assumption that its DRNG output is only as strong as the root key's own
secrecy; anyone who can derive this GPG key can derive the encryption subkey and decrypt every backup made under it.
It does not change who can spend funds: this key has no signing authority over the wallet itself, only over its own
backups.

'''Decryption does not authenticate the descriptor's source or intended use.''' Encrypting to a public key, by itself,
proves nothing about who produced the plaintext: OpenPGP's public encryption subkey is, as its name states, public, so
anyone who obtains it (a keyserver publication, a leaked ciphertext's key ID, or any other disclosure) can construct a
fresh, validly-encrypted backup addressed to it while choosing an arbitrary descriptor as the payload. An AEAD tag or
OpenPGP's integrity mechanism authenticates that the ciphertext was not modified after encryption; it does not
authenticate that the plaintext came from the wallet owner or matches the wallet's actual policy. This matters
specifically for recovery: a recovered descriptor can carry archived output state, and an implementation that treats
successful decryption as sufficient to resume address generation could be steered onto an attacker-chosen policy.
Implementations SHOULD treat a decrypted descriptor as untrusted until it is checked against an independent
commitment to the intended wallet or policy (for example, a previously-recorded fingerprint of the expected
descriptor), and SHOULD import it inactive rather than immediately generating or watching addresses from it.
Implementations that want the OpenPGP layer itself to establish provenance MAY additionally sign the backup with a
key the recipient already trusts, rather than relying on encryption alone.

==Reference implementation==

None yet. RSA-GPG key derivation and GPG subkey import are implemented by existing BIP-0085 tools; encrypting and
decrypting the resulting descriptor is ordinary OpenPGP usage requiring no new code beyond deriving the key and
invoking existing tooling.

==Open questions==

This is an early draft; the choices above have not yet had review. In particular:

* Should this document define a non-GPG, minimal hybrid-encryption alternative for constrained devices, rather than
  depending on full OpenPGP support?
* Does encrypting to a single RSA key, with no additional passphrase, provide adequate protection on its own, or
  should this document recommend an additional layer for the ciphertext at rest?
* Should this document normatively require a signature (see Privacy and security considerations) rather than leaving
  it optional?

